  "id": "Compound::DB00477 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpromazine's interaction with Chrm1 is supported by multiple evidence pathways, all of which converge on the role of G-protein coupled receptors in modulating cholinergic signaling. The drug binds to Hrh4, which is involved in the activation of G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, a pathway that is also engaged by G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity. This suggests that Chlorpromazine may influence the function of Chrm1 through a mechanism involving the modulation of G-protein signaling. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Chlorpromazine can interact with different receptors (Hrh4 and Chrm3) to affect the same downstream pathway, highlighting a potential role in regulating cholinergic signaling. The consistent involvement of G-protein coupled receptor activity in the pathways suggests a mechanistic link between Chlorpromazine and Chrm1, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
